import{_t as e,it as t,n,rt as r}from"./chunks/framework.B5d4da8a.js";var i=JSON.parse('{"title":"Plugin System in handl Itself","description":"","frontmatter":{"title":"Plugin System in handl Itself","type":"adr","date":"2026-09-02T00:00:00.000Z","status":"proposed","status_note":"Proposed\\n\\nRefines [ADR-0047 — Meta Functions as `plugin-lib-*` Crates](./0047-meta-functions-as-plugin-lib-crates.md)\\n(hands the meta functions to handl as callable modules, which this ADR then _uses_ from handl).\\nRelated: [ADR-0044 — Language as Data and Runtime Split](./0044-language-as-data-and-runtime-split.md),\\n[ADR-0046 — Capability Discipline at the Language Level](./0046-capability-discipline-at-the-language-level.md)."},"headers":[],"relativePath":"handl/project/adrs/0050-plugin-system-in-handl.md","filePath":"vendored/handl/project/adrs/0050-plugin-system-in-handl.md"}'),a={name:`handl/project/adrs/0050-plugin-system-in-handl.md`};function o(n,i,a,o,s,c){return e(),r(`div`,null,[...i[0]||=[t(`<h1 id="adr-0050-plugin-system-in-handl-itself" tabindex="-1">ADR-0050: Plugin System in handl Itself <a class="header-anchor" href="#adr-0050-plugin-system-in-handl-itself" aria-label="Permalink to “ADR-0050: Plugin System in handl Itself”">​</a></h1><h2 id="context" tabindex="-1">Context <a class="header-anchor" href="#context" aria-label="Permalink to “Context”">​</a></h2><p>The platform&#39;s composition model — <code>Plugin</code>, <code>Operation</code>, <code>Behavior&lt;Op&gt;</code>, <code>RuleAcceptor</code>, <code>ContributionContext</code>, <code>PluginRegistry</code>, <code>Host</code> — is today implemented entirely in Rust (<code>crates/platform/src/</code>). Concrete operations (<code>Format</code>, <code>Lint</code>, <code>Eval</code>, <code>Compile</code>, …) are Rust traits with Rust rule acceptors (<code>BasicFormatter</code>, <code>BasicLinter</code>, …). Rules are Rust structs implementing <code>Behavior&lt;Op&gt;</code>.</p><p>This works, but it puts the <em>first application of handl</em> — its own tooling — in Rust. Every new format/lint rule is a Rust crate, a recompile, and a host edit. The language&#39;s own toolchain cannot be extended by writing handl. That is the opposite of the platform&#39;s stated goal (&quot;every concept is a <code>Plugin</code>, every tool is a <code>Host</code> that composes plugins&quot;) when the plugins themselves can only be Rust.</p><p>The question this ADR answers: <strong>can the plugin system itself — registry, contribution, dispatch, the rule acceptors, and the fmt/lint rules — be handl, with Rust reduced to a permanent bootstrap kernel?</strong></p><h3 id="what-handl-already-has-verified" tabindex="-1">What handl already has (verified) <a class="header-anchor" href="#what-handl-already-has-verified" aria-label="Permalink to “What handl already has (verified)”">​</a></h3><ul><li><code>if</code> — conditionals.</li><li>Named bindings + lambdas → recursion.</li><li><code>str</code>, <code>array</code>, <code>object</code>, <code>fn</code> modules — string building, collection traversal, higher-order combinators.</li><li>AST payload is <code>serde_json::Value</code> — the AST is already JSON-shaped at the Rust boundary.</li><li><code>plugin-loader-wasm</code> / <code>plugin-loader-native</code> — handl scripts can already be loaded as plugins.</li></ul><p>handl is <strong>not</strong> missing control flow, recursion, or string building. The gap is narrower than it first appears.</p><h3 id="what-handl-is-missing-verified" tabindex="-1">What handl is missing (verified) <a class="header-anchor" href="#what-handl-is-missing-verified" aria-label="Permalink to “What handl is missing (verified)”">​</a></h3><ol><li><strong>AST exposure.</strong> There is no <code>ast.parse(source)</code> module callable from handl today. <code>plugin-lib-parse</code>&#39;s <code>LibraryPlugin</code> registers nothing (its <code>contribute</code> is empty). The parser is Rust (<code>plugin-engine-parse-pratt</code>) and its output does not cross into handl.</li><li><strong>A rule-contribution path.</strong> handl scripts can be loaded as plugins, but there is no bridge that lets a handl script contribute <code>FormatBehavior</code>/<code>LintBehavior</code> rules into a <code>BasicFormatter</code>/<code>BasicLinter</code> acceptor, nor an equivalent acceptor written in handl.</li><li><strong>A binding façade.</strong> <code>ModuleRuntime</code> + <code>ModuleRule</code> is the binding mechanism, but exposing a new Rust library today means writing a full <code>plugin-lib-*</code> crate per library. There is no generic &quot;register this Rust fn as that handl module function&quot; primitive.</li></ol><h3 id="the-two-permanent-rust-pieces" tabindex="-1">The two permanent Rust pieces <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-two-permanent-rust-pieces" aria-label="Permalink to “The two permanent Rust pieces”">​</a></h3><p>A self-hosted toolchain does not mean zero Rust. Every self-hosted language keeps a kernel runtime in a host language (CPython in C, V8 in C++, rustc in Rust with LLVM in C++). Porting the <em>evaluator</em> to handl would only make handl slower running handl — no gain. The win is porting the <strong>toolchain</strong> (parser, formatter, linter, type-checker), not the engine.</p><p>This ADR therefore fixes two permanent Rust pieces and moves everything above them into handl:</p><ol><li><strong>Bootstrap engine</strong> — <code>plugin-engine-exec-*</code> (tree-walk / bytecode / cranelift). handl cannot run without a runtime. The engine is the kernel that stays Rust.</li><li><strong>Binding lib</strong> — a generic extern façade so any Rust crate is exposed as a handl module through one uniform primitive. Mostly exists (<code>ModuleRuntime</code> + <code>ModuleRule</code>); needs a clean generic surface so a new Rust binding is not a full <code>plugin-lib-*</code> crate.</li></ol><p>Everything above the line — stdlib, the plugin system, parser, fmt, lint, rules — becomes handl.</p><h2 id="decision" tabindex="-1">Decision <a class="header-anchor" href="#decision" aria-label="Permalink to “Decision”">​</a></h2><h3 id="_1-two-permanent-rust-layers-everything-above-is-handl" tabindex="-1">1. Two permanent Rust layers; everything above is handl <a class="header-anchor" href="#_1-two-permanent-rust-layers-everything-above-is-handl" aria-label="Permalink to “1. Two permanent Rust layers; everything above is handl”">​</a></h3><div class="language-"><button title="Copy code" data-copied="Copied" class="copy"></button><span class="lang"></span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes handl-dark github-light" style="--shiki-dark:#ffffff;--shiki-light:#24292e;--shiki-dark-bg:#2a2a2a;--shiki-light-bg:#fff;" tabindex="0" dir="ltr"><code><span class="line"><span>Rust (permanent):</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>  1. bootstrap engine   — plugin-engine-exec-* (tree-walk/bytecode/cranelift)</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>  2. binding lib        — generic extern façade over ModuleRuntime + ModuleRule</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>handl (everything above the line):</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>  stdlib          — str, array, object, fn, ... (uses bindings where needed)</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>  plugin system   — registry, contribute, dispatch, host.load (pure handl)</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>  parser          — port to handl (phase 2; may use bindings for lexer primitives)</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>  fmt + lint      — rule acceptors + per-kind rules (pure handl, extensible)</span></span></code></pre></div><p>The engine stays Rust because porting it yields only a slower handl. The binding lib stays Rust because it is the seam between handl and the host language&#39;s library ecosystem — that seam is inherently host-language code. Nothing else needs to be Rust.</p><h3 id="_2-the-plugin-system-is-a-handl-module-not-a-rust-crate" tabindex="-1">2. The plugin system is a handl module, not a Rust crate <a class="header-anchor" href="#_2-the-plugin-system-is-a-handl-module-not-a-rust-crate" aria-label="Permalink to “2. The plugin system is a handl module, not a Rust crate”">​</a></h3><p>The generic plugin system — <code>PluginRegistry</code>, <code>ContributionContext</code>, <code>RuleAcceptor</code>, <code>Operation</code>, <code>Behavior</code>, <code>Host.load</code> fixpoint — is implemented as a <strong>handl module</strong> (<code>plugin.*</code>) backed by handl data and handl functions. No new <code>plugin-lib-plugin</code> Rust crate holds the <em>logic</em>; the only Rust involved is the binding façade that exposes whatever host primitives the handl module needs (e.g. a stable <code>ast.parse</code> callable).</p><p>The Rust trait model maps to handl data with no loss of expressiveness, because handl is JSON + functions:</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Rust</th><th>handl</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>Plugin</code> trait</td><td>object <code>{id, capabilities, contribute, run}</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>Operation</code> marker type</td><td>string id (<code>&quot;fmt&quot;</code>, <code>&quot;lint&quot;</code>)</td></tr><tr><td><code>Behavior&lt;Op&gt;</code> trait</td><td>object <code>{kind, apply}</code>; <code>apply</code> is a fn mutating a request</td></tr><tr><td><code>RuleAcceptor</code> trait</td><td>a plugin whose <code>run</code> dispatches to behaviors by <code>kind</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>PluginRegistry</code></td><td>handl object: map of capability → plugin</td></tr><tr><td><code>ContributionContext</code></td><td>handl object passed to <code>contribute</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>Host</code> + fixpoint load</td><td>handl <code>host.load</code> function: register → contribute → resolve → repeat to fixpoint</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Traits are not needed on the handl side because dispatch is by string <code>kind</code> over JSON requests, which is exactly what the Rust <code>BasicFormatter</code>/<code>BasicLinter</code> already do internally (<code>for rule in &amp;self.rules { if rule.kind() == kind { rule.apply(&amp;mut req)?; } }</code>). The handl version is the same loop written in handl.</p><h3 id="_3-fmt-and-lint-are-handl-rule-acceptors-with-per-kind-rule-files" tabindex="-1">3. <code>fmt</code> and <code>lint</code> are handl rule acceptors with per-kind rule files <a class="header-anchor" href="#_3-fmt-and-lint-are-handl-rule-acceptors-with-per-kind-rule-files" aria-label="Permalink to “3. fmt and lint are handl rule acceptors with per-kind rule files”">​</a></h3><p><code>fmt</code> and <code>lint</code> become handl modules, each providing a rule acceptor that dispatches by <code>AstNode.kind</code> over the AST-as-JSON. Rules are handl files — one per AST kind — that register into the acceptor:</p><div class="language-"><button title="Copy code" data-copied="Copied" class="copy"></button><span class="lang"></span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes handl-dark github-light" style="--shiki-dark:#ffffff;--shiki-light:#24292e;--shiki-dark-bg:#2a2a2a;--shiki-light-bg:#fff;" tabindex="0" dir="ltr"><code><span class="line"><span># rules/fmt/call.hnl   — formats a \`call\` node</span></span>
<span class="line"><span># rules/fmt/var.hnl    — formats a \`var\` node</span></span>
<span class="line"><span># rules/lint/unused.hnl — lints for unused bindings</span></span></code></pre></div><p>Adding a rule is adding a handl file. No Rust change, no recompile, no host edit. This is the extensibility property the Rust model promised but could only deliver at Rust-compile time.</p><p>The <code>FormatRequest</code> / <code>LintRequest</code> contracts (currently undesigned — <code>plugin-fmt-expression</code> returns <code>NotImplemented</code>) are defined <strong>as handl data shapes</strong> while the first rules are written. Early churn is expected and accepted; the contract hardens as the rule set grows.</p><h3 id="_4-handle-fmt-lint-dispatches-to-the-handl-tooling-kit" tabindex="-1">4. <code>handle fmt|lint</code> dispatches to the handl tooling kit <a class="header-anchor" href="#_4-handle-fmt-lint-dispatches-to-the-handl-tooling-kit" aria-label="Permalink to “4. handle fmt|lint dispatches to the handl tooling kit”">​</a></h3><p><code>host-cli</code> gains <code>fmt</code> and <code>lint</code> subcommands. They are thin: they eval the handl &quot;tooling kit&quot; (a kit that loads the <code>plugin</code>/<code>fmt</code>/<code>lint</code> modules and the rule files) and call the handl <code>fmt</code>/<code>lint</code> entry points with the target source. The CLI does not implement formatting or linting; it hosts the handl that does.</p><h3 id="_5-build-order" tabindex="-1">5. Build order <a class="header-anchor" href="#_5-build-order" aria-label="Permalink to “5. Build order”">​</a></h3><ol><li><strong>Binding lib façade (Rust).</strong> Generic extern module registration; one primitive to expose a Rust fn as a handl module function.</li><li><strong>Expose <code>ast.parse</code> / <code>ast.lex</code> via the façade.</strong> Parser stays Rust for now; this makes the end-to-end loop work (handl can read a source file&#39;s AST).</li><li><strong>handl <code>plugin</code> module.</strong> Registry, contribute, dispatch, <code>host.load</code> fixpoint — pure handl, mirroring the Rust model in the table above.</li><li><strong>handl <code>fmt</code> + <code>lint</code> modules.</strong> Rule acceptors with kind-dispatch over AST JSON.</li><li><strong>handl rule files.</strong> One per AST kind; add without touching the language.</li><li><strong>Wire <code>handle fmt|lint</code> in <code>host-cli</code>.</strong> Eval the tooling kit, call the entry points, print results.</li><li><strong>(Later phase) Port the parser to handl.</strong> Rust shrinks to engine + bindings. The <code>ast.parse</code> binding becomes a fallback / fast path, not the only path.</li></ol><p>Steps 1–2 are the bootstrap. Steps 3–6 are the deliverable. Step 7 is the eventual self-hosting reward, not the starting line.</p><h2 id="consequences" tabindex="-1">Consequences <a class="header-anchor" href="#consequences" aria-label="Permalink to “Consequences”">​</a></h2><h3 id="positive" tabindex="-1">Positive <a class="header-anchor" href="#positive" aria-label="Permalink to “Positive”">​</a></h3><ul><li><strong>First application of handl is handl.</strong> The language&#39;s own toolchain — formatter, linter, and the plugin system that hosts them — is written in the language. This is the strongest possible dogfooding and the clearest proof of the platform&#39;s composability claim.</li><li><strong>Rules are extensible without touching the language.</strong> A new format or lint rule is a <code>.hnl</code> file. No Rust crate, no recompile, no host edit. The Rust model promised this at Rust-compile time; the handl model delivers it at file-add time.</li><li><strong>The plugin system is agnostic / decoupled / extensible / modular / composable / replaceable</strong> — because it is handl data + functions, and rules are just more handl registering into it. Swapping the whole plugin system is swapping a handl module.</li><li><strong>Rust shrinks to a permanent, small kernel.</strong> Engine + bindings. Everything above is auditable, editable, and replaceable as handl. The surface area of &quot;things you must know Rust to change&quot; becomes minimal.</li><li><strong>The contract hardens under real use.</strong> <code>FormatRequest</code> / <code>LintRequest</code> get designed while writing the first real rules, against real AST shapes, in the language that will consume them.</li><li><strong>Bindings unlock the Rust ecosystem.</strong> handl stdlib can use mature Rust crates (unicode, regex, …) where handl stdlib is thin, without reimplementing them. Self-hosting the toolchain logic does not require reimplementing every library.</li></ul><h3 id="trade-offs" tabindex="-1">Trade-offs <a class="header-anchor" href="#trade-offs" aria-label="Permalink to “Trade-offs”">​</a></h3><ul><li><strong>A bootstrap bridge must be written and maintained.</strong> The binding façade and the <code>ast.parse</code>/<code>ast.lex</code> exposure are Rust and must track engine/parser changes. This is small and stable compared to the toolchain, but it is non-zero.</li><li><strong>Performance.</strong> A tree-walk-evaluated formatter over large files will be slower than a Rust formatter. Mitigations: bytecode/cranelift engines for the tooling kit, or a fast Rust <code>ast.parse</code> path that returns a compact JSON shape. Not a day-1 concern; revisit when the rule set is complete and real files are being formatted.</li><li><strong>Self-hosting trap.</strong> Every handl syntax change can break the formatter, which is written in the language just changed. True of every self-hosted toolchain (gofmt, rustfmt partially, GHC) and survivable: the bootstrap engine and the Rust <code>ast.parse</code> fallback keep the toolchain runnable while handl syntax is in flux.</li><li><strong>Contract churn early.</strong> <code>FormatRequest</code> / <code>LintRequest</code> are designed alongside the first rules. Expect revisions until the rule set covers the AST. This is intentional — designing the contract in an immature language against real rules is more honest than designing it in the abstract — but it means early rule files will be rewritten.</li><li><strong>Parser port (step 7) is deferred, not free.</strong> Full self-hosting (no Rust parser) is a later phase with its own ADR. This ADR delivers a self-hosted <em>toolchain</em> on top of a Rust parser; the parser port is the next milestone, not part of this decision.</li></ul><h2 id="references" tabindex="-1">References <a class="header-anchor" href="#references" aria-label="Permalink to “References”">​</a></h2><ul><li><a href="./0047-meta-functions-as-plugin-lib-crates.html">ADR-0047 — Meta Functions as <code>plugin-lib-*</code> Crates</a> — provides <code>lex.lex</code> / <code>parse.parse</code> / <code>eval.eval</code> as handl-callable modules, which this ADR&#39;s binding façade generalizes and which the handl <code>plugin</code> module uses.</li><li><a href="./0044-language-as-data-and-runtime-split.html">ADR-0044 — Language as Data and Runtime Split</a> — the data/runtime split that makes the AST JSON-shaped and the engine pluggable.</li><li><a href="./0046-capability-discipline-at-the-language-level.html">ADR-0046 — Capability Discipline at the Language Level</a> — capability discipline that the handl plugin system inherits.</li><li><code>crates/platform/src/plugin.rs</code> — the Rust <code>Plugin</code> / <code>PluginRegistry</code> model this ADR ports to handl.</li><li><code>crates/platform/src/format.rs</code>, <code>crates/platform/src/lint.rs</code> — the Rust <code>BasicFormatter</code> / <code>BasicLinter</code> rule acceptors the handl <code>fmt</code> / <code>lint</code> modules mirror.</li><li><code>crates/platform/src/plugin_model.rs</code> — <code>Contribution</code>, <code>RuleAcceptor</code>, <code>Operation</code>, <code>Behavior</code>, <code>ContributionContext</code> — the composition primitives ported to handl data.</li></ul>`,41)]])}var s=n(a,[[`render`,o]]);export{i as __pageData,s as default};